<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
        The ONE network permission, added 2026-09-19 on founder direction:
        "Live rates for FX converter, suggest reliable API and implement. Lets
        fix the data privacy later on."

        It was in the DEBUG manifest only, which is why a live fetch would have
        worked on the founder's emulator and failed silently in a real build.

        Salapify makes exactly one request, from lib/data/fx_service.dart, to
        open.er-api.com, with no key, no account, no identifying header and no
        body. Nothing about the user's money leaves the device.

        THREE THINGS THIS BREAKS, and all three are for before the store
        rather than for today:
          - the app's own header says "Offline Only";
          - main.dart's doc comment says there is no network call anywhere;
          - Play's data safety form has to declare it.
        They are written up in docs/reviews/fx-live-rates.md rather than left
        for somebody to rediscover in a rejection email.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>
    <!--
        NO CLOUD BACKUP OF THE LEDGER. Decided 2026-09-19 on the founder's
        instruction to have the security and privacy reviewer settle it.

        Android's default is allowBackup="true", which quietly copies the app's
        files to the user's Google Drive. The reason that is refused here is
        not the encryption, which is genuinely good: it is that the copy is one
        Salapify cannot see, cannot verify and cannot delete. The honest answer
        to "delete everything you hold about me" has to be one sentence,
        "uninstall the app", and a Drive copy turns it into a treasure hunt
        through Google One settings.

        It also is not only the user's own data. Debt.person is free text
        naming whoever owes them money, and those people consented to nothing.

        THE COST IS REAL AND IS ACCEPTED: with this off and no export built
        yet, a lost phone means the data is gone. That is precisely why the
        export is a launch blocker rather than something Auto Backup is
        allowed to stand in for.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="Salapify"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="false">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
